  "notes": [
    "EMBED menuCategories + items inside Restaurant: The menu is tightly owned by the restaurant, always read together for browsing (REQ-8 to REQ-12), and mutated exclusively by its manager (REQ-34, REQ-35). A single restaurant menu in a single-city v1 launch is bounded (realistic cap of ~20 categories × ~30 items = 600 items), well under MongoDB's 16 MB document limit. This makes the restaurant-browse query a single document fetch with no $lookup.",
    "SNAPSHOT items inside Order: Order items embed a copy of name, unitPrice, and lineTotal at placement time (REQ-23). This is intentional — menu prices can change after an order is placed, and financial audit, dispute resolution (REQ-41), and commission calculation (BR-5) all require the price at time of order, not the current menu price.",
    "REFERENCE Restaurant from Order / Cart / Delivery: Restaurant is an independent aggregate queried across many contexts (browse, order history, admin search). Orders and carts hold the restaurantId reference plus a denormalised restaurantName snapshot for display without a join.",
    "REFERENCE DeliveryAgent from Order and Delivery: Delivery agents are independently managed (REQ-39), have their own lifecycle (approval, suspension), and their profile is updated continuously (live location). Embedding would make the restaurant or order document a moving target.",
    "SEPARATE Delivery collection: Delivery has its own status machine, assignment-attempt history, and a growing agentLocationHistory array (REQ-26, REQ-28, REQ-29). Embedding it in Order would make the Order document unbounded in size and would require frequent partial updates to a deeply nested path.",
    "SEPARATE Payment collection: Payment maps 1-to-1 with an Order but contains gateway-specific fields, refund sub-records, and PCI-sensitive tokens. Keeping it separate limits exposure surface and makes it straightforward to scope access in middleware without touching Order documents.",
    "SEPARATE Cart collection: Cart is transient and updated on every item add/remove/quantity change. Embedding it in Customer would create a high-churn hotspot on the Customer document. A dedicated carts collection with a compound unique index (customerId + restaurantId) enforces the one-cart-per-customer-per-restaurant rule (REQ-13, REQ-14) at the database level.",
    "SEPARATE Review collection: Reviews are written once but read independently (admin moderation REQ-33, rating aggregation REQ-32). The unique index on orderId enforces BR-6 (one review per order) at the database level. avgRating and reviewCount on Restaurant are denormalised counters updated by a post-save hook or change-stream worker after each review insert or isVisible toggle.",
    "Customer savedAddresses are EMBEDDED in the customers document: Addresses are tightly owned, always read with the customer profile, and have a natural small upper bound (REQ-6 implies a handful of saved addresses). The deliveryAddress on Order is a further snapshot copy taken at placement to preserve the historical delivery location.",
    "statusHistory is EMBEDDED in Order: It is append-only, bounded by the finite order status machine (~7 transitions max), and always read with the order for tracking and audit purposes.",
    "assignmentAttempts is EMBEDDED in Delivery: Bounded by the small number of retries before an order is escalated; read together with delivery status for ops dashboards.",
    "Decimal128 is used for all monetary fields (price, subtotal, deliveryFee, taxAmount, total, commissionAmount, refund amounts) to avoid floating-point rounding errors in financial calculations.",
    "The platformConfigs collection uses a key/value pattern to store admin-configurable parameters (REQ-42) such as defaultCommissionRate, defaultDeliveryRadiusKm, accountLockDurationMinutes, sessionTokenTTLHours, agentOfferTimeoutSeconds. The Mixed value type accommodates numeric, boolean, and string config values.",
    "agentLocationHistory inside Delivery is a capped-style array suitable for v1. For production scale, consider offloading live location streams to a time-series collection or Redis; the history embedded here serves the proof-of-delivery and ETA recalculation use cases at launch volume.",
    "The sparse unique indexes on users.email and users.phone accommodate users who register with only one of the two identifiers (REQ-1).",
    "RBAC is enforced at the application/middleware layer using the role field on User; no separate permissions collection is modelled as the four roles are fixed and defined in the requirements."
  ],
  "datastore_product": "MongoDB"
}